# OpenSpec first — hard stop (no exceptions)

Every change with **observable product behaviour** starts as an OpenSpec change under `openspec/changes/`, **before** any implementation, smoke, Roadmap Done, or release tag.

CI enforces this: `scripts/check-openspec-gate.sh` fails the Forgejo Test workflow when `crates/` behaviour sources change without a touch under `openspec/changes/` or `openspec/specs/`.

## STOP before code

Before the **first** `Write` / `StrReplace` / commit that touches product behaviour under:

`crates/app`, `crates/app-base`, `crates/app-state`, `crates/app-call-model`, `crates/calling`, `crates/browser`, `crates/session`, `crates/chatsvc`, `crates/graph`, `crates/core`, `crates/webview`

you MUST already have:

1. `openspec/changes/<name>/` with `proposal.md`, `design.md`, `tasks.md`, and spec deltas (English only — rule `openspec-english`)
2. A green `openspec validate <name>` (CLI: `/usr/local/bin/openspec`, rule `openspec-cli-path`)

If you already edited crates without that: **stop implementing**. Create and validate the OpenSpec change, then continue. Do not «finish the code and add OpenSpec later».

## Required flow

1. Plan step 1 = OpenSpec change (`/opsx-propose`). A Cursor plan does **not** replace it.
2. Implement with `/opsx-apply`; check off `tasks.md` including verify.
3. After merge on the fork: `/opsx-archive` (deltas into `openspec/specs/`).
4. Only pure refactoring, tooling, or docs with **no** behaviour change may use `skip_specs: true` — and must still create the change folder so the CI gate sees `openspec/changes/`.

## Do not

- Write behaviour code first and archive OpenSpec after the release
- Skip OpenSpec because upstream already has a PR, or because «it is only a fix»
- Skip OpenSpec because the feature already has code and «only needs smoke»
- Mark Roadmap Done or cut a release tag for behaviour without archived (or active) OpenSpec coverage
- Claim OpenSpec was done when only ROADMAP/CHANGELOG/ADR changed

## Agent self-check (every turn that edits product code)

Ask: «Does `openspec/changes/<name>/` exist for this work and did `openspec validate` pass?» If no → create it before the next code edit.
